Blue-Green Deployment:
You have two identical environments: Blue (current live) and Green (new version).
Users are routed to Blue while you deploy the new version in Green.
Once Green is tested, you switch the traffic from Blue → Green.
Before update:
Users → Blue (Live)

Deploy new version to Green:
Users → Blue (Live)
Green (Test)

Switch traffic:
Users → Green (Live)
Blue (Idle)

Advantages:
Zero downtime
Easy rollback: If Green fails → switch back to Blue
Safer deployments

Tools:
Jenkins pipeline can automate deployment to Blue/Green
Load balancers handle traffic switching

Zero-Downtime Updates:
Purpose: Keep the application live while updating it.

How it works:

Instead of stopping the old version, you start the new version first.
Traffic is gradually shifted to new version.
Old version removed only after new version is stable.

Advantages:
Users never see downtime
Supports continuous deployment
Use case: Updating live websites or APIs

Docker Compose:
Purpose: Manage multi-container applications easily.

How it works:
You define all containers, networks, and volumes in a docker-compose.yml file.
Run everything with one command.

Advantages:
Easy multi-container orchestration
Reproducible environments
Good for local development & small production apps



